{{ assign "pub_date" "2026-11-20" }}
{{ assign "title" "リロられる is a fun word" }}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        {{> writing_head_boilerplate
            title=title
            pub_date=pub_date
            description="Amusing-to-me observation on the passive form of a Japanese slang verb."
        }}
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    </head>
    <body>
        {{> writing_header}}
        <main>
            <article itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article">
                <h1 itemprop="headline"><span lang="ja">リロられる</span> is a fun word</h1>
                {{> writing_byline_dateline author="Arjun Satarkar" pub_date=pub_date}}
                <div itemprop="articleBody">
                    <p>
                        While discussing the passive form of Japanese verbs with a friend, we noticed that the suffix used to form them (for some verb classes), <span lang="ja">られる</span> (<i>rareru</i>), contains three of the five characters in the 'r' row of the Japanese syllabary (the <span lang="ja">ら<ruby>行<rt>ぎょう</rt></ruby></span>), and so the natural question became "can we somehow construct a verb form that uses the other two as well?"
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Searching <a href="https://jisho.org/">jisho.org</a> for plausible candidates yielded the slang term <span lang="ja">リロる</span> (<i>riroru</i>), meaning "to reload" (derived from an abbreviation of the English word "reload"), which seems to see some use online. Its passive form would indeed be the wonderful <span lang="ja">リロられる</span> (<i>rirorareru</i>), a word making complete use of—and solely composed of—the 'r' row of the kana chart. It's fun to say out loud too!
                    </p>
                    <p>That's all for this one.</p>
                </div>
            </article>
        </main>
        {{> writing_footer}}
    </body>
</html>
